WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
A distributed denial-of-service event is an availability problem first.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:09.300
The immediate symptom is that a public service becomes slow, unstable,

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.960
or unreachable because some part of its path is under pressure.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.900
That pressure might be network capacity at the edge,

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.160
connection or state handling in an infrastructure component,

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.260
or expensive behavior inside the application itself.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.680
In this course we keep those categories defensive and high level.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.940
The goal is not to study how hostile traffic is created.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.880
The goal is to understand what defenders need to keep real services operating when traffic

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.300
is abnormal,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.480
intense, or intentionally disruptive.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.180
DDoS mitigation is the set of preparation and response capabilities that help a team

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.360
absorb,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:50.180
filter, redirect, rate limit, cache, or gracefully degrade under that pressure.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.000
It is not one product and it is not one checkbox.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:59.020
A CDN may help for web traffic, a WAF may help with application requests,

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:04.360
a DNS provider may protect name resolution, an Anycast edge may distribute load,

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:09.700
and a scrubbing provider or upstream provider may filter large traffic before it reaches

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:10.880
the origin.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.500
Those tools matter, but only when the architecture, routing, contacts, telemetry,

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.360
and runbooks are ready before the incident begins.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.620
The preparation is often more important than the emergency button.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.880
Teams need an inventory of public services, dependencies, owners, provider contracts,

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.900
emergency contacts, and change procedures.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.400
They need baselines that show what normal traffic looks like,

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.100
dashboards that support fast classification,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.480
and escalation paths that do not depend on one person being awake.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.860
They also need clear assumptions: which services must remain available,

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.480
which paths can degrade, and which emergency controls are acceptable for the business.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.740
During an event, a good runbook reduces confusion.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.520
It gives responders a sequence: confirm the signal, classify the pressure,

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.940
protect critical paths, engage providers, apply scoped controls, communicate clearly,

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.120
preserve evidence, and recover carefully.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.100
The better the preparation, the less the team has to invent under pressure.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.040
DDoS mitigation is availability engineering with security context, operational discipline,

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.740
and calm communication built in.
